msgctxt "11"
msgid "スキル３偽名テキスト"
msgstr ""

#. max 6143 bytes
msgctxt "12"
msgid "Karna appears in the Indian epic \"The Mahabharata\"\nas a hero on the vanquished side.\n(The Mahabharata's central conflict is a power struggle\nbetween the Pandava and Kaurava royal families.)\n\nKarna is famous as the rival of Arjuna, a great hero\nin Hindu mythology.\n\n             ◆\n\nKarna was born from the daughter of a human king, Kunti,\nand the sun god, Surya.\n\nKunti was a wife of the Kuru King Pandu, but he was under\na curse preventing him from having children, so each of his\nqueens had no choice but to bear children by other means.\n\nKunti was a woman who had a mantra that allowed her to\ncopulate with and bear the child of any god and thus\ngive Pandu children in that manner.\n\n……but. Before she became the king's wife, she tested\nthat mantra and bore a single child. That child was Karna,\nthe golden hero born via relations with the sun god, Surya.\n\nKunti was a determined woman and, afraid of her first birth\nand anxious if the god acknowledged his own child,\nprayed to Surya \"I want proof that this child is your son.\"\n\nSurya heard Kunti's words and gave unto the child,\nupon his birth, his own power and attributes.\n\nThat was the origin of the golden armor that\nrendered Karna invulnerable.\n\n             ◆\n\nBut. Even shown such grace and sincerity, Kunti abandoned\nher first son. For her, to be a queen of Kuru King Pandu, \nthe existence of her son naught but unnecessary.\n\nThat is how Karna, abandoned by his own mother, came to be\nignorant of his own birth, but lived with only the things\ngiven by his father, the sun god Surya, held in his chest.\n\nKarna's form, ignorant of his mother's face and unsure if\nher motives were dishonest, couldn't be called beautiful.\n\nWhile he had his father's brilliant authority, his form was\nstained black. His countenance was cruel, and every action\nwas violent. Because of he lacked a human mother, he didn't\nlearn the subtleties of human emotion and spent his days\nconsidered a nuisance by the people around him.\n\nThat was how Karna grew up, but he didn't resent his mother\nor his surroundings. Instead, he accepted everything.\n\n\"I was given life by my mother and father.\n No matter how my mother was, I hold no contempt for her.\n If there is anyone I hate, that I hold contempt for,\n it is for myself alone.\"\n\nContrary to appearance, Karna was an exceedingly virtuous\nand perceptive child. It's likely because he was the child\nof a god, as well as someone without a single relative.\nKarna was blessed with the opportunity to examine the weak,\ntheir lives, and their value. As a result, he chose to walk\na path of purity of his own free will.\n\n\"I who was born with more than others should show\n a 'proof of life' greater than them. Unless I do so,\n those without power will never be rewarded.\"\n\nKarna simply had the conviction to live without dishonoring\nhis father's prestige or shaming those who compensated him.\n\"Ruthless and relentless, yet also full of majesty,\"\nwas Karna's stance taken shape.\n\n             ◆\n\nAnd so Karna, as a young man, participated in a conference\nfor the Kuru family. At this conference, the five Pandava\nbrothers indulged in their prided and famed martial arts.\nThe third son Arjuna's archery was especially magnificent,\nto the point that he was praised as having no equal.\n\nWhen the grounds united as one voice seeking someone to\nequal the Pandavas, Karna leapt in to participate\nand displayed martial arts on the same level as Arjuna's.\n\n(As an aside, the reason the unmotivated Karna challenged\n Arjuna is famous yet ambiguous. It was later confirmed\n to be because Arjuna was the only opponent that Karna,\n who never envied or hated anyone, was conscious of.)\n\nKarna challenged Arjuna to determine superiority.\nBut to challenge Arjuna, a member of the royal family,\none must be a Kshatriya or higher.\n\n(*Kshatriya……\n Those of the military or royalty in the caste system. \n Karna seemed a Vaishya (merchant) or Shudrya (slave).)\n\nKarna, his challenge refused due to a difference in status,\nwas a laughingstock. The one who saved Karna from this was\nthe eldest of the Kaurava clan's hundred princes,\nwho opposed the Pandavas, Duryodhana.\n\nHe took interest in Karna and made him a king on the spot.\nKarna was saved from disgrace, but then his foster father,\nhearing his promotion, appeared to establish his origins.\n\nThe five Pandava brothers further ridiculed Karna,\nwho displayed martial arts superior to their own, saying\n\"The son of a coachman should have a sense of shame.\"\n\nThese words enraged Karna.\nIf it were about himself, he would be resigned and accept,\nbut he would not allow insults toward his foster father.\n……even if it was a foster father who made himself known\nout of greed, to Karna, he was the father who raised him,\nwho Karna should take pride in.\n\nThe conflict between Karna and the five Pandava brothers\nhad become something he couldn't back down from,\nbut sunset had come and the conference came to an end.\n\nFrom then on, Karna, as the friend of Duryodhana,\nwho saved him and treated him as a king, lived as a\nguest of honor of the Kaurava clan's hundred princes.\n\n\nWhat awaited him was a cruel battle beyond comprehension\nwith the five Pandava brothers―with the great hero Arjuna."
msgstr ""

