#. max 6255 bytes
msgctxt "32"
msgid "\nThe conflict of the Kauravas, for whom Karna held his bow\nas a soldier, and the Pandavas led by Arjuna, intensified\nand finally led to war with territory on the line.\n\nThe Kurukshetra War, where Karna's life came to an end.\n\n             ◆\n\nKarna continued to wield his strength for the victory of\nthe hundred Kaurava princes, and Duryodhana.\nOn the Pandava side, only Arjuna could oppose Karna,\nbut even he knew facing Karna directly meant death.\n\nThrough a number of conflicts, fates, and mutual hatred,\nthe war between the two camps arrived at Kurukshetra.\n\n\nHaving reached this point, Karna's mother, Kunti,\nwagered one last gamble: reveal Karna's lineage to him\nand draw him to the Pandava camp.\n\nKunti confided in Krishna, a friend of Arjuna in the guise\nof an attendant, and the two went to meet with Karna.\n\nKunti revealed herself as Karna's mother, speaking in tears\nof how fighting with his blood brothers was futile, and\nthat by fighting alongside Arjuna, he would attain glory.\n\nKarna made sure to thank his archenemy Arjuna's friend,\nKrishna, and calmly listened to his mother's arguments.\nAfterwards,\n\n\"I understand your words. To join hands with my brothers\n and return to my proper form.\n It is a story filled with light, without a single fault.\"\n\nThen, facing the rejoicing Kunti,\nKarna continued speaking in an even softer voice.\n\n\"But I wish for you to answer one thing.\n Do you not feel those words of yours were too late?\"\n\nShe was too late in declaring herself as his mother.\nShe was too late in looking back on Karna.\n\"If you do not feel ashamed, then please answer.\n ―――If you, who call yourself my mother, have done\n no wrong, then accept the past without shame.\"\n\nWhile Kunti was a selfish woman,\nit came from her innocence and naiveté;\nshe was most definitely not a shameless woman.\nShe knew what she had done……\nabandoning the newborn Karna for her own sake……\nwas completely selfish, and felt remorse for it.\n\nThat was why she had some degree of pride.\nShe could not tell Karna, who grew up alone until now,\nwho was grateful to his foster family and not once\nbrought himself to hate another, such a horrible lie.\n\nWithout giving an answer, Kunti broke off negotiations.\nAs she was taking her leave, Karna said:\n\n\"It is a fraudulent, selfish love.\n The only one that love can save is yourself.\n That love is directed at no one but yourself.\n However―――\"\n\n\"I shall answer those feelings.\n I will not kill the brothers who cannot best me in battle.\n I will only give my fullest against my archenemy, Arjuna.\"\n\nKarna swore not to lay a hand on the brothers\nwho were inferior to him in strength.\nThe countless times afterward that Karna\nspared the five brothers were due to this oath.\n\n\"Return to the place you obtained by your own hands.\n ……though it was only once,\n I am grateful that you called me your son.\"\n\nKarna opened the gates and escorted Kunti out.\nThat was the love Karna bore for his mother, Kunti.\nNot love for his mother at this late hour, but Kunti's \nresolution to appeal with \"her feelings as a mother\"……\nEven if it wasn't genuine…… that is what he answered.\nKunti ran the risk of her past coming to light.\nTo the Hero of Charity, Karna,\nsuch determination was worthy of compensation.\n\n             ◆\n\nAnd so, it was just before the final battle.\nArjuna's father, the thunder god Indra, realized that Karna\ncould not be persuaded, so he took the form of a Brahmin,\nmade contact with Karna, and took his golden armor.\n\nThough Karna had lost the invulnerability given by\nhis father Surya, he still refused to relent from battle.\nHe cared not that, with the loss of his golden armor,\nhe hastened his own death.\n\nStruck by Karna's resolution, Indra asked why.\nWhy would he continue to fight with the loss of his armor,\nyet without blaming Indra, who hatched this scheme\nout of love for Arjuna?\n\n\"There is no need to hate you.\n It was simply a matter of being one step ahead.\n In fact―――yes. Though a god, you are also a father.\n Such a thing brings me joy.\"\n\nThen why continue to the battlefield, asked Indra.\n\n\"To me, defeat is bringing shame upon my father.\n Even if death awaits me, I cannot run away.\"\n\nAfter all, that was his reason for living.\nTo Karna, who lived his life with pride in those who\ngave him life and raised him, his life was not his own.\n\n\"Besides, I owe Duryodhana a debt. For some reason, \n that audacious, yet timid man is so bright to me. \n It may be blasphemy against my father but, at times, \n I feel that light of his as the warmth of the sun.\"\n\nIt wasn't the fire of the sun that Karna carried,\nnor the absolute brilliance of Surya; to Karna,\nthat sun was the imperfect charm that humans displayed.\n\nIndra, seeing in him the divinity of Surya himself,\ngave Karna his spear. He had taken from this noble hero\nsomething greater than his life.\nUnless he gave something as compensation,\nhis honor would be stained, and above all―――\nhe was charmed by him.\nIf it was this man, he could wield the mightiest spear\nthat Indra hadn't given even to his own son.\n\n             ◆\n\nKarna left the Brahmin, having lost his armor (flesh),\nand headed off to the battlefield, thin as a ghost.\n\nTo his final battle with Arjuna.\nKarna was already without allies; his trusted charioteer\nwas already an enemy, in collusion with the Pandavas.\n\nNumerous burdens, feelings for his younger half-brothers.\n\nFrom a curse, the wheel of Karna's chariot was stuck.\nThe string on Arjuna's bow was drawn to its limits.\nThe brothers, long since bound by an unseen fate\nto vie for supremacy, could only in this moment\nstrike at one another with all their might.\n\n\n――And sure enough, Arjuna's bow shot down the sun.\n\n\n\n             ◆\n\nAfter death, Karna is said to have unified with his father,\nSurya. He was \"The Hero of Charity,\" a saint whose creed\nwas not to refuse people who ask or rely on him.\n\nThe hero who, while extraordinarily capable,\nwas tragically enemies with his blood brothers,\ncast with various curses, and lost his life\nwithout showing his true worth―that is Karna.\n\nAs a side note, whether or not Arjuna knew that\nKarna was his older brother is…… uncertain.\n\nIt seems the only people who knew that Karna\nwas Kunti's son were Karna, Kunti, Krishna,\nand the sun god, Surya.\n"
msgstr ""

